--TEST--
an expression `catch`'s arms are clauses of one guard, tried in order, and what none of them matched leaves the frame carrying the same object — `rule:expressions/catch-expression` and `rule:expressions/catch-lowers-to-block-form`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:statements/catch-as-an-expression
// `rule:expressions/catch-expression`: the arms are clauses of the one guard rather than guards of
// each other, so the chain is the block form's "any number, tried in order;
// the first whose class matches runs" — a supertype written first shadows the
// arms after it, exactly as it does there.
class Fail {
    public static function io(): int {
        throw new IOError("io");
    }
}

// The first arm's class does not match; the second's does.
echo Fail::io() catch (LogicError $wrong) => 1 catch (IOError $right) => 2, "\n";
// `IOError` is a `RuntimeError`, so the supertype written first takes it and
// the arm after it is dead.
echo Fail::io() catch (RuntimeError $super) => 3 catch (IOError $shadowed) => 4, "\n";

// § 6: what no arm matched is handed straight back, so the exception leaves
// the expression as the very same object it arrived with — including the
// `location` its own `throw` wrote.
try {
    echo Fail::io() catch (LogicError $wrong) => 5, "\n";
} catch (IOError $out) {
    echo "rethrown: ", $out->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
2
3
rethrown: io
